## TL;DR  
*`text‑anchor`* and *`symbol‑placement`* are **not** reliable clues that a layer is a route‑shield.  
The property that **actually makes a symbol a shield** is **`icon‑text‑fit`** (and its companion `icon‑text‑fit‑padding`).  
A robust heuristic is:

```js
const isShield = (layer) => {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout ?? {};

  // 2️⃣ The layer must be trying to fit text inside an icon
  const textFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (!textFit || textFit === 'none') return false;   // missing or default → not a shield

  // 3️⃣ The icon‑image must be a stretchable badge (optional but very helpful)
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  if (typeof iconImage === 'string' && !iconImage.includes('{')) {
    // plain static name – could still be a shield, but we can double‑check the sprite later
  }

  // 4️⃣ The layer usually supplies a text field that contains the road number
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (typeof textField === 'string' && !/\{ref\}|\{network\}/.test(textField)) {
    // No reference number → unlikely to be a shield
    return false;
  }

  // 5️⃣ (optional) The icon‑image expression often contains a pattern like
  //    "{network}_{ref_length}" or "road_{ref_length}"
  if (typeof iconImage === 'string' && !/\{.*\}/.test(iconImage)) {
    // No placeholder → probably a plain POI icon
    return false;
  }

  // If we got here the layer looks like a shield
  return true;
};
```

Below is a full explanation of **why** the original heuristic fails and **what** you should key off instead.

---

## 1. Why the current heuristic is unsound

| Heuristic | What the spec says | Why it mis‑classifies |
|-----------|-------------------|----------------------|
| `layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined` | `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when omitted. The spec treats *absence* as “use the default”. | Many non‑shield symbol layers (place‑names, POIs, etc.) also omit `text-anchor`. Absence therefore tells you nothing about “shield‑ness”. |
| `layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'` | `symbol-placement` defaults to `"point"` for symbol layers. It only matters for *text* placement relative to the point geometry (e.g. `"line"` for road‑labels). | Almost every point‑based symbol layer (including POIs, city labels, etc.) has the default value `"point"`. Using it as a filter captures *all* those layers. |
| Combined test | Both conditions are true for the overwhelming majority of point‑symbol layers. | The intersection is still huge, so you end up tagging *everything* that looks like a point symbol as a shield, and you miss the few layers that *do* use a shield but happen to override one of those defaults (e.g. `symbol-placement: "point"` is explicit, `text-anchor: "center"` is explicit). |

**Bottom line:** Neither property is a *signal* of a shield; they are merely *defaults* that many layers share.

---

## 2. What *does* make a symbol a **route shield**?

### 2.1 `icon-text-fit` (the decisive flag)

* **Purpose** – tells the renderer to stretch the icon so that the text (the road number) fits inside it.
* **Values** – `"none"` (default), `"width"`, `"height"`, `"both"`.
* **Shield layers** – always set this to `"both"` (or at least `"width"`). Without it the badge would be drawn at its native size and the number would overflow.

> **Spec reference:** <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/layers/#icon-text-fit>

### 2.2 `icon-text-fit-padding`

* Gives the padding around the text inside the badge. Most shield styles set a non‑zero padding array, e.g. `[1,4,1,4]`. The presence of a padding array (or a non‑default value) reinforces the shield hypothesis.

### 2.3 `icon-image` that contains **place‑holders**

* Shields are usually generated from a *template* that pulls data from the feature properties:
  * `{network}_{ref_length}`
  * `road_{ref_length}`
  * `{ref}` (rare, but sometimes the whole ref is baked into the name)
* The placeholder (`{…}`) tells you the icon name is **dynamic**, i.e. it will resolve to a different sprite entry for each road number.

### 2.4 `text-field` that contains a reference number

* Most shield layers also expose the road number as a text label, e.g. `"{ref}"` or `"{ref} {network}"`. If the layer never renders any text, it is unlikely to be a shield.

### 2.5 Stretchable sprite metadata (optional but useful)

* In the sprite JSON, a shield badge is usually marked with:
  * `stretchX` / `stretchY` arrays,
  * `content` rectangle,
  * `pixelRatio` (often `2` for @2x).
* If you have access to the sprite JSON you can verify that the referenced image has these fields. This is a *post‑load* check, not something you can see in the style alone, but it can be used for a final validation step.

---

## 3. A practical, low‑cost heuristic for a **style‑audit script**

Below is a step‑by‑step checklist you can implement in JavaScript (or any language that can read the style JSON). It works **without** loading the sprite files, so it is fast enough for a static audit.

```js
function isPotentialShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 0️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout ?? {};

  // 1️⃣ icon-text-fit must be set and not "none"
  const textFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (!textFit || textFit === 'none') return false;

  // 2️⃣ icon-text-fit-padding should be present (optional but strong signal)
  const padding = layout['icon-text-fit-padding'];
  if (!Array.isArray(padding) || padding.length !== 4) {
    // No padding → still possible, but we treat it as weaker evidence
    // (you could decide to reject here if you want stricter filtering)
  }

  // 3️⃣ The icon-image should be a template (contains `{` and `}`)
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  if (typeof iconImage !== 'string' || !iconImage.includes('{')) {
    // static icon name → almost certainly NOT a shield
    return false;
  }

  // 4️⃣ The text-field should reference a road number (ref, network, etc.)
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (typeof textField !== 'string' ||
      !/\{ref\}|\{network\}|\{ref_length\}/.test(textField)) {
    // No reference number in the label → unlikely to be a shield
    return false;
  }

  // 5️⃣ (optional) If you have the sprite JSON, verify that the referenced
  //    image has stretch/content metadata.
  //    This step is omitted here because the audit script may not have the sprite.

  // All checks passed → treat as a shield
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it catches | Why it’s reliable |
|-------|----------------|-------------------|
| `icon-text-fit` ≠ `"none"` | Guarantees the layer is trying to *fit* text inside an icon. | Only shields (or other “badge‑with‑text” patterns) need this. |
| `icon-image` contains `{}` | Ensures the icon name is **dynamic** (different per feature). | POI icons are static (`"cafe"`, `"park"`). |
| `text-field` contains `{ref}` / `{network}` | Confirms the layer is actually displaying a road number. | Place‑name layers use `{name}` or similar, not `{ref}`. |
| `icon-text-fit-padding` present | Reinforces that the author cared about the badge’s interior spacing. | Most shield styles set a non‑zero padding; plain icons rarely do. |

If you want to be *even more* strict, you can add:

* `layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true` (shields often allow overlap because they’re small).
* `layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true` (some shield styles ignore placement to keep numbers visible).

---

## 4. Edge‑cases & Gotchas

| Situation | Why the simple heuristic might mis‑fire | How to handle it |
|-----------|------------------------------------------|------------------|
| **Custom “badge” icons that are *not* road shields** (e.g. city‑logo badges) | They also use `icon-text-fit` and placeholders. | Look at the placeholder pattern: shields usually reference `{ref}` or `{network}`. Badges often use `{name}` or a static string. |
| **Shield layers that use a *static* icon name** (rare, pre‑generated per‑ref images) | No `{}` in `icon-image`. | If you have the sprite JSON, check that the icon name matches the pattern `us-interstate_2`, `road_3`, etc. If you cannot inspect the sprite, you may need a whitelist of known static shield names. |
| **Layers that set `icon-text-fit` via an expression** | The value may be an expression object, not a plain string. | Detect expression objects (`Array.isArray(layout['icon-text-fit']) && layout['icon-text-fit'][0] === 'case'` etc.) and evaluate whether any branch resolves to a non‑`none` value. For a static audit you can treat any expression as “potentially a shield” if it contains the literal `"none"` *and* another value. |
| **`symbol-placement` set to `"line"` but still a shield** (e.g., shields placed along a road line) | The original heuristic would have excluded it. | Do **not** use `symbol-placement` as a filter. Shields can be placed on points (`"point"`) *or* on lines (`"line"`). |
| **`text-anchor` explicitly set to `"center"`** (same as default) | The original script considered `undefined` only. | Again, ignore `text-anchor`. Its value is irrelevant for shield detection. |

---

## 5. Updating Your Audit Script

1. **Replace the old condition**  

   ```js
   // Old (incorrect)
   if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
       layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
       // flagged as shield
   }
   ```

2. **Insert the new helper** (or inline the logic)

   ```js
   if (isPotentialShieldLayer(layer)) {
       // flagged as shield – correct
   }
   ```

3. **Add a fallback for expression values** (optional)

   ```js
   function resolveIconTextFit(value) {
     // If it's a plain string, return it.
     if (typeof value === 'string') return value;
     // If it's an expression array, try to find a non‑'none' literal.
     if (Array.isArray(value)) {
       // Very naive: look for any string that is not 'none'
       for (const part of value) {
         if (typeof part === 'string' && part !== 'none') return part;
       }
     }
     return 'none';
   }

   // In the helper:
   const textFit = resolveIconTextFit(layout['icon-text-fit']);
   if (textFit === 'none') return false;
   ```

4. **Log why a layer was (or wasn’t) classified** – helpful for debugging.

   ```js
   console.log(`Layer ${layer.id} shield check:`, {
     textFit,
     iconImage: layout['icon-image'],
     textField: layout['text-field'],
   });
   ```

---

## 6. Quick Reference Cheat‑Sheet

| Property | Expected value for a **shield** | Typical value for a **non‑shield** |
|----------|----------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| `type` | `"symbol"` | `"symbol"` (both) |
| `layout.icon-text-fit` | `"both"` (or `"width"`/`"height"`) **not** `"none"` | `undefined` or `"none"` |
| `layout.icon-text-fit-padding` | Array of 4 numbers (often `[1,4,1,4]`) | `undefined` or default `[0,0,0,0]` |
| `layout.icon-image` | String **containing** `{` and `}` (e.g. `"us-interstate_{ref_length}"`) | Plain name like `"cafe"` or `"park"` |
| `

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that shows how to

1. **Create a draggable pin (marker)** at a known longitude/latitude.  
2. **Open a popup** when the user clicks the pin (or taps it on mobile).  
3. Keep the popup in sync with the marker after it is dragged.

The code uses only the public MapLibre GL JS API – no custom sprite or source is required. If you want to use your own pin image you can replace the default marker with a custom HTML element or an image URL (see the “Custom icon” section).

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin + popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">

  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">

  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }

    /* Optional: style a custom HTML marker */
    .custom-marker {
      width: 30px;
      height: 30px;
      background: url('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pin.svg') center/contain no-repeat;
      cursor: grab;
    }
    .custom-marker:active { cursor: grabbing; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Save the above as `index.html` and create a separate `app.js` file (or embed the script inline).*

---

## 2️⃣  JavaScript – `app.js`

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Initialise the map
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],     // lon, lat – San Francisco for demo
  zoom: 12,
  // If you self‑host a style, make sure the sprite & glyph URLs are reachable.
});

// Add navigation controls (optional but handy)
map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Create a draggable marker (pin)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// ---- 2a. Using the built‑in default marker (blue pin) ----
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  draggable: true,          // <‑‑ makes it draggable
  // If you want a custom HTML element instead of the default pin:
  // element: document.createElement('div').classList.add('custom-marker')
})
  .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574]) // initial position
  .addTo(map);

// ---- 2b. (Optional) Custom HTML marker example ----
// const el = document.createElement('div');
// el.className = 'custom-marker';
// const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
//   .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574])
//   .addTo(map);

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Create a popup that will be shown on click
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
  closeButton: true,
  closeOnClick: false,   // keep it open when the map is clicked elsewhere
  offset: 25              // distance from the marker tip
})
  .setHTML('<h3>My draggable pin</h3><p>Click the map to move me.</p>');

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Wire up the interactions
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// 4a – Show the popup when the marker is clicked
marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // Prevent the map's click handler from also firing
  e.stopPropagation();

  // Position the popup at the marker's current coordinates
  popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat()).addTo(map);
});

// 4b – (Optional) Keep the popup anchored while dragging
//     This makes the popup follow the pin as you drag it.
marker.on('drag', () => {
  // If the popup is already open, move it with the marker
  if (popup.isOpen()) {
    popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat());
  }
});

// 4c – Close the popup when the user clicks elsewhere on the map
map.on('click', () => {
  popup.remove();
});

// 4d – Example: update the popup content after drag ends
marker.on('dragend', () => {
  const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
  popup.setHTML(
    `<h3>Pin moved!</h3>
     <p>New position:<br>lon: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}</p>`
  );
  // Re‑open the popup at the new location (optional)
  popup.setLngLat(lngLat).addTo(map);
});
```

### What each block does

| Block | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Map init** | Loads a style, sets centre/zoom, adds navigation UI. |
| **Marker** | `new maplibregl.Marker({draggable:true})` creates a pin that can be moved with the mouse or touch. |
| **Popup** | `new maplibregl.Popup()` creates a reusable popup object. |
| **Click on marker** | `marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', …)` opens the popup at the marker’s current coordinates. |
| **Drag → popup follow** | `marker.on('drag', …)` moves the popup while the pin is being dragged (so the UI feels fluid). |
| **Map click** | Closes the popup when the user clicks anywhere else. |
| **Drag end** | Updates the popup content (or any other UI) after the pin is dropped. |

---

## 3️⃣  Customising the pin (optional)

If you don’t like the default blue pin, you have two easy ways:

### A. Use an image URL (no HTML)

```js
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
  draggable: true,
  // The `element` option is omitted – we use the built‑in <img> element.
})
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .setPopup(popup)               // you can also attach a popup directly
  .addTo(map);

// Then replace the default image via CSS:
.mapboxgl-marker img {
  content: url('https://example.com/my-pin.png');
  width: 30px; height: 30px;
}
```

### B. Provide a full HTML element (most flexible)

```js
const el = document.createElement('div');
el.className = 'custom-marker';   // style defined in the <style> block above
// You could also set `el.innerHTML = '<svg …>'` for an inline SVG.

const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .addTo(map);
```

Both approaches work with the same drag‑and‑popup logic shown earlier.

---

## 4️⃣  Things to watch out for

| Issue | Why it matters | Fix / tip |
|-------|----------------|-----------|
| **CORS on custom sprite / image** | If you host a custom PNG/SVG for the marker, the server must send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your domain) otherwise the browser blocks it. | Serve the image from the same origin as the page, or enable CORS on the static host (GitHub Pages, Netlify, S3, etc.). |
| **MapLibre version** | The `draggable` flag has been stable since v1.0, but the **multiple‑sprite** feature (if you later need custom icons from a sprite) only exists from v3.0 onward. | Use the latest `3.x` release (the CDN link above points to 3.2.0). |
| **Touch devices** | Dragging works out‑of‑the‑box, but a quick tap may be interpreted as a drag‑start. | The default `drag` tolerance is 5 px; you can adjust it with `marker.setDraggable(true)` and `marker.dragging` events if needed. |
| **Popup staying open after drag** | If you want the popup to stay open while dragging, you must manually move it (see the `drag` listener). | The example already does that. |
| **Multiple markers** | If you later add many draggable pins, keep a reference to each marker/popup pair (e.g., an array) and attach the same listeners in a loop. | See the “Scaling up” note below. |

---

## 5️⃣  Scaling up – many draggable pins

If you need **more than one** draggable pin, store each marker/popup pair in an array:

```js
const pins = [
  { lngLat: [-122.447, 37.753], label: 'A' },
  { lngLat: [-122.45, 37.75],   label: 'B' },
];

pins.forEach((p) => {
  const m = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
    .setLngLat(p.lngLat)
    .addTo(map);

  const pop = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
    .setHTML(`<h3>Pin ${p.label}</h3>`);

  m.getElement().addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    e.stopPropagation();
    pop.setLngLat(m.getLngLat()).addTo(map);
  });

  m.on('drag', () => pop.isOpen() && pop.setLngLat(m.getLngLat()));
  m.on('dragend', () => console.log(`Pin ${p.label} moved to`, m.getLngLat()));
});
```

All the same behaviours (drag, popup follow, click‑to‑open) are preserved.

---

## 6️⃣  Full working example (single file)

If you prefer a **single HTML file** for quick testing, copy‑paste this:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – draggable pin + popup</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    body,html{margin:0;padding:0;height:100%;}
    #map{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}
    .custom-marker{
      width:30px;height:30px;
      background:url('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pin.svg') center/contain no-repeat;
      cursor:grab;
    }
    .custom-marker:active{cursor:grabbing;}
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  // ---- marker (use default or custom HTML) ----
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.className = 'custom-marker';
  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
    .setLngLat([-122.447303, 37.753574])
    .addTo(map);

  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
    .setHTML('<h3>Draggable pin</h3><p>Click the map to close.</p>');

  // click on marker → open popup
  marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', e => {
    e.stopPropagation();
    popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat()).addTo(map);
  });

  // keep popup anchored while dragging
  marker.on('drag', () => {
    if (popup.isOpen()) popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat());
  });

  // close popup when clicking elsewhere
  map.on('click', () => popup.remove());

  // optional: update content after drag ends
  marker.on('dragend', () => {
    const ll = marker.getLngLat();
    popup.setHTML(
      `<h3>Pin moved!</h3>
       <p>lon
